(Regression test: `is`/`is not` on text/buffer operands must compare
 CONTENT, not pointers. Previously `cmp rax, rbx` compared raw
 addresses, so a runtime string (e.g. from arguments's first) could
 never match an equal-content literal - two equal-content strings are
 essentially never at the same address since add_string mints a fresh
 label per literal occurrence.)

a text called user_command is arguments's first.
If user_command is "reboot" then, print "text var equals literal: MATCH".
Otherwise, print "text var equals literal: no match [{user_command}]".

If user_command is not "shutdown" then, print "text var not-equal literal: correctly differs".

a buffer called linebuf is "reboot".
If linebuf is "reboot" then, print "buffer equals literal: MATCH".
If linebuf is "shutdown" then, print "buffer equals literal: WRONGLY matched shutdown".

(NULL-safety: arguments's second is absent in this test invocation -
 _get_arg returns NULL for an out-of-range index. The comparison must
 evaluate to false, not dereference the NULL pointer.)
a text called missing is the argument at 5.
If missing is "anything" then, print "NULL comparison wrongly matched".
Otherwise, print "NULL-safe: absent argument correctly not-equal".
